0.022186279296875, 0.03466796875, -0.0120086669921875, -0.0177001953125, 0.013092041015625, -0.020751953125, -0.006435394287109375, -0.0003287792205810547, 0.00791168212890625, 0.0097198486328125, 0.0107879638671875, -0.018646240234375, 0.00855255126953125, 0.027984619140625, 0.021331787109375, -0.0008234977722167969, -0.027496337890625, -0.004878997802734375, -0.007320404052734375, 0.01513671875, -0.014739990234375, -0.0048065185546875, -0.00843048095703125, -0.02923583984375, 0.0029354095458984375, 0.0047607421875, -0.0008296966552734375, -0.00832366943359375, 0.0372314453125, 0.017852783203125, 0.01067352294921875, -0.026214599609375, 0.001461029052734375, 0.01091766357421875, 0.0008287429809570312, -0.000820159912109375, 0.00222015380859375, -0.021881103515625, 0.0032520294189453125, -0.01540374755859375, -0.0088958740234375, -0.023529052734375, -0.02606201171875, 0.027862548828125, -0.052490234375, -0.0006680488586425781, -0.0251617431640625, -0.0273590087890625, 0.022216796875, -0.030242919921875, -0.0012197494506835938, -0.006717681884765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work addresses the following research questions: (1) To what extent do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit robustness, as measured by performance variance and failure modes, when exposed to stochasticity and changes in task order? (2) What specific factors contribute to their observed fragility, and how can evaluation protocols be improved to better capture these issues?\n\n**Background.** We focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Augmented Agents (EMAA), which store and retrieve textual summaries of past episodes to inform future decisions, and (b) Self-Reflective Language Agents (SRLA), which maintain a log of self-generated reflections and lessons learned after each task. EMAA emphasizes episodic recall, while SRLA focuses on meta-cognitive self-assessment. These methods were selected for their contrasting approaches to memory construction and their prominence in recent benchmarks. Prior evaluations have primarily used fixed task sequences, often without reporting variance or sensitivity to task order.\n\n**Methods.** We conduct a comprehensive re-evaluation of EMAA and SRLA along two axes: (1) running each agent on five independent random seeds to quantify performance variance and identify failure modes, and (2) randomly shuffling the order of tasks (drawn from a suite of 10 multi-step reasoning and manipulation tasks) to assess the effect of task order. Tasks were selected to span both symbolic reasoning (e.g., text-based puzzle solving) and embodied manipulation (e.g., object arrangement in simulated 3D environments), ensuring diversity in required skills and environment feedback. For memory analysis, we manually inspect the agents\u2019 memory banks after each run, using a rubric that codes for specificity (level of detail), relevance (applicability to future tasks), and completeness (coverage of key events). Two independent annotators performed the coding, and inter-annotator agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa (average \u03ba = 0.81). Disagreements were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached.\n\n**Results.** Our experiments reveal two key findings that expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks: across five runs, mean final task success rates ranged from 62% to 78% (95% confidence intervals \u00b16%), with standard deviation in final task success rates ranging from 8% to 15%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise, occasionally leading to catastrophic forgetting or spurious memory entries. Second, the agent\u2019s improvement is highly dependent on task order: in some permutations, agents failed to improve on later tasks, with average performance drops of up to 20% (mean drop: 13%, p < 0.05, paired t-test) compared to the default curriculum order. Prior works often adopt default orderings that impose an implicit curriculum, acting as a hidden prerequisite for success.\n\nManual examination of the agents\u2019 memory indicates that task and environment underspecification contribute to this fragility: memory entries often lack sufficient detail to generalize across task variations, and ambiguous environment feedback leads to misleading or incomplete memories. To validate this, we introduced a more detailed memory rubric (requiring explicit mention of task goals, actions, and outcomes) and provided agents with structured environment feedback (e.g., explicit success/failure signals and clarifying messages). This intervention was operationalized by modifying the memory-writing module and environment API. The impact was measured by comparing performance degradation in shuffled task orders before and after the intervention: average degradation reduced from 20% to 10% (mean post-intervention: 8%, 95% CI \u00b13%), but significant gaps still remain.\n\n**Discussion.** These persistent gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as limitations in memory retrieval mechanisms, insufficient exploration, or unmodeled environment stochasticity\u2014may also contribute to fragility. Future work should disentangle these factors by, for example, systematically ablating retrieval strategies, varying exploration parameters, or introducing controlled environment randomness to isolate their effects. Hypotheses such as \u201cretrieval failures disproportionately affect transfer to novel tasks\u201d or \u201cincreased environment stochasticity amplifies memory errors\u201d could be tested in targeted experiments.\n\n**Recommendations.** We advocate for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, including: (1) reporting mean and variance of results across multiple random seeds, (2) systematically varying and reporting task order effects, (3) providing statistical significance tests for observed differences, and (4) offering detailed breakdowns of failure cases. For effective human oversight, we recommend developing interfaces that allow users to audit, edit, and flag agent memories (e.g., with inline annotations or approval workflows), and incorporating human-in-the-loop feedback to catch underspecified or misleading entries before they propagate.\n\nIn summary, our work highlights the need for transparent, robust evaluation and oversight in memory-based self-improving agents, and provides concrete steps\u2014such as improved reporting standards and memory auditing tools\u2014toward more reliable deployment in complex environments."}}